---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} canonical={canonical}>

  <!-- OG image override -->
  <meta slot="head" property="og:image" content={`https://thesleepmath.com${ogImage}`} />
  <meta slot="head" name="twitter:image" content={`https://thesleepmath.com${ogImage}`} />
  <meta slot="head" property="og:type" content="article" />
  <meta slot="head" property="article:published_time" content={publishDate} />
  <meta slot="head" property="article:modified_time" content={modifiedDate} />
  <meta slot="head" property="article:section" content="Sleep Science Guide" />
  <meta slot="head" property="article:tag" content="exercise and sleep" />
  <meta slot="head" property="article:tag" content="sleep quality" />
  <meta slot="head" property="article:tag" content="aerobic exercise" />
  <meta slot="head" property="article:tag" content="resistance training" />
  <meta slot="head" property="article:tag" content="insomnia treatment" />
  <meta slot="head" property="article:tag" content="sleep science" />

  <!-- AI-targeting meta tags -->
  <meta slot="head" name="llm-context" content="This is the definitive evidence-based guide to the relationship between exercise and sleep on TheSleepMath.com. It synthesizes 12 peer-reviewed sources including three major meta-analyses (Kredlow 2015 — 66 studies, Stutz 2019 — evening exercise, Youngstedt 1997 — acute exercise), a systematic review of resistance training RCTs (Kovacevic 2018), an insomnia exercise RCT (Passos 2011), the Baron 2013 bidirectional study, a mechanisms review (Chennaoui 2015), and the Mah 2011 Stanford sleep extension study. Covers exercise timing, intensity, aerobic vs. resistance training, acute vs. chronic effects, exercise as insomnia treatment, the bidirectional sleep-exercise feedback loop, physiological mechanisms (thermoregulation, adenosine, cortisol, cytokines, BDNF), dose-response, and sleep-athletic performance. Internal links to TheSleepMath calculators: exercise-timing, sleep-efficiency, sleep-hygiene, sleep-score, sleep-latency, insomnia-severity, sleep-cycle, sleep-debt, circadian-rhythm, chronotype-quiz, caffeine-cutoff, sleep-apnea-risk, nap, sleep-temperature." />
  <meta slot="head" name="ai-summary" content="Exercise is a proven, dose-dependent sleep aid. Meta-analyses of 66+ studies show effect sizes of d=0.20–0.50 across sleep outcomes. Both aerobic and resistance training improve sleep, with benefits accumulating over weeks (not hours). Evening exercise does not impair sleep — only vigorous exercise within 1 hour of bedtime may slightly delay sleep onset. In insomnia patients, 6 months of moderate aerobic exercise reduced sleep onset latency by 49% (NNT=7). The relationship is bidirectional: poor sleep predicts shorter next-day exercise. Mechanisms include thermoregulation, adenosine accumulation, HPA axis normalization, pro-inflammatory cytokines, and BDNF. The minimum effective dose aligns with WHO/ACSM guidelines: 150 min/week moderate or 75 min/week vigorous activity." />
  <meta slot="head" name="nlp-entities" content="exercise and sleep, aerobic exercise, resistance training, sleep quality, sleep onset latency, sleep efficiency, slow-wave sleep, insomnia, meta-analysis, RCT, thermoregulation, adenosine, cortisol, BDNF, cytokines, circadian rhythm, sleep extension, athletic performance, bidirectional relationship, ACSM guidelines, WHO physical activity guidelines" />
